Requirements: 10   with obligations: 10

[task-01] Runs on Claude should reuse cached prompt openings wherever requests repeat
    one, and a run that does not need its answers straight away should be able to send
    its model calls through the Message Batches API at half price.
    -> claude-reuse-cached-prompt-openings  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       For runs on Claude, when multiple requests share a repeated prompt opening
       (prefix), the requests reuse the cached prompt opening via Anthropic prompt
       caching instead of resending it uncached.
    -> cache-reuse-claude-only  [boundary/inferred, code_and_tests]
       Prompt-opening cache reuse applies to runs on Claude; runs on other models are
       not given Claude-specific caching directives.
    -> batch-api-routing  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A run that does not need its answers straight away can send its model calls
       through the Message Batches API instead of the synchronous Messages API.
    -> batch-results-returned-to-calls  [functional/inferred, code_and_tests]
       Each model call sent through the Message Batches API gets back its own result,
       matched to the originating call once the batch completes, so the run proceeds as
       it would with a synchronous answer.
    -> immediate-runs-stay-synchronous  [regression/inferred, code_and_tests]
       Runs that need their answers straight away continue to send model calls
       synchronously, with batch routing applied only to runs that do not need immediate
       answers.

[task-02] The shortest opening the client marks as reusable follows Anthropic's current
    documented minimum for each model: 512 tokens for Claude Sonnet 5.5, Opus 5.5 and
    Haiku 5.5. The module documentation stops saying that Anthropic rejects a breakpoint
    below the minimum; a short prefix is simply not cached.
    -> min-reusable-prefix-512-tokens  [boundary/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The shortest opening (prefix) the client marks as reusable is 512 tokens for each
       of Claude Sonnet 5.5, Opus 5.5 and Haiku 5.5, matching Anthropic's documented
       minimum for those models. A prefix shorter than 512 tokens is not marked as
       reusable, and one of 512 tokens or more is eligible.
    -> module-doc-no-rejection-claim  [docs_config/explicit, code_only]
       The module documentation no longer states that Anthropic rejects a cache
       breakpoint placed below the model's minimum token count; it instead describes
       that a prefix shorter than the minimum is simply not cached.
       why code_only: This is a documentation text change that a reader confirms by
       reading the module docstring; no test would add evidence.
    -> short-prefix-not-cached  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A prefix shorter than the model's minimum cacheable length is simply not cached;
       it is not treated as an error, and the module documentation describes this
       behavior.

[task-03] The defect-to-test pair mapping stage, which sends the most calls in a review,
    already places its defect list in the reusable opening, yet a recent review on
    Claude Sonnet 5.5 showed no cache reads on any stage. Repeated pair-mapping calls
    that share an opening are read from the cache.
    -> pair-mapping-defect-list-in-reusable-opening  [invariant/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Preserve the placement of the defect list in the reusable opening of every
       defect-to-test pair mapping call, so that the defect list is part of the opening
       that is identical across the repeated calls of that stage.
    -> sonnet-5-5-cache-reads-reported  [functional/inferred, code_and_tests]
       When a review runs on Claude Sonnet 5.5, repeated calls that share a reusable
       opening are served from the prompt cache, so cache reads are reported on the
       stages that repeat such calls, including defect-to-test pair mapping. The recent
       review's finding of no cache reads on any stage must no longer hold.
    -> pair-mapping-shared-opening-cache-reads  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Repeated defect-to-test pair-mapping calls that share the same reusable opening
       are served from the prompt cache, so that cache reads occur on the second and
       later calls.

[task-04] The mutation descriptor stage sends one call per defect, and defects that
    share a region repeat that region's numbered source in every call. That source moves
    into the reusable opening so calls over the same region share it.
    -> descriptor-one-call-per-defect  [invariant/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The mutation descriptor stage sends exactly one call for each defect, so the
       number of calls equals the number of defects, including when several defects
       share a region.
    -> region-source-in-shared-opening  [functional/inferred, code_and_tests]
       Defects that share a region get that region's numbered source from the reusable
       opening, which is common to all calls over that region. Each per-defect call
       carries the source through that shared opening and does not repeat it in its own
       call-specific content.

[task-05] A run can opt in to sending its model calls through the Message Batches API.
    The setting belongs to the run's configuration and is off by default, so `acceptance
    check` and `acceptance decompose` keep calling the model directly. Benchmark scoring
    and experiment scripts can switch it on.
    -> batch-api-opt-in  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A run can opt in to sending its model calls through the Message Batches API; when
       opted in, the run's model calls are submitted via the Message Batches API rather
       than direct calls.
    -> batch-setting-in-run-config  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The opt-in setting for sending model calls through the Message Batches API is
       defined as part of the run's configuration, so that a run's configuration
       determines whether batching is used.
    -> batch-api-off-by-default  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The run configuration setting that opts a run into sending model calls through
       the Message Batches API defaults to off, so `acceptance check` and `acceptance
       decompose` call the model directly unless the setting is explicitly enabled.
    -> batch-setting-enableable-by-scripts  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Benchmark scoring and experiment scripts can enable the opt-in Message Batches
       API setting in a run's configuration, so that the run's model calls are sent
       through the Message Batches API.

[task-06] How a call was sent does not change what was asked: a batched request is
    recorded under the same key as the same request sent directly, and its recording
    replays with no live call. The recording notes that it was sent through a batch. Two
    recorded runs over the same input still produce byte-identical review state
    whichever way their calls were sent.
    -> batched-request-same-recording-key  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A batched request is recorded under the same key as the same request sent
       directly, so the recording key is independent of whether the call was sent
       through a batch or directly.
    -> batched-recording-replays-offline  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A recording made from a batched request replays without making any live call.
    -> recording-notes-batch-origin  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A recording of a request that was sent through a batch carries a note indicating
       that it was sent through a batch.
    -> review-state-byte-identical-across-send-modes  [invariant/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Two recorded runs over the same input produce byte-identical review state,
       whether their calls were sent directly or through a batch, including when the two
       runs use different send modes.

